comments.message:
> Concretely what changes do you think need made to the application to make a flexible design? 

To confirm the requirement that hardware initialization is not limited to 'before FruDevice starts' but may need to happen any time a new hardware is added to the system. Including the custom handler functions.

The system being made up of a 'fixed' hardware and `0 or more` blades / expansion cards.

> I said selection of init. This design has no concept of a "device". If things can be modeled as a "device" that's detectable, I agree, that's where the line should end for this and entity-manager/reactors should begin. In the examples in the repo today, compile-time selection is not just a fixed case of detection.

There is a difference in compiled kernel modules, target SoC, devicetree, application support and such.

But that does not need to limit us in the overall architecture?

> There are no distinction for "devices" here. There is a known image that does known steps to init the platform its loaded on, no runtime detection.

We can simply assume that the image is running on the right hardware (otherwise it would make no sense to run hardware specific init functions) and therefore we have 'detected' it.

I think we are running in circles here. My point is to generalize the design and not think in rigid categories like 'platform init'. **The platform is just a device which cannot be removed, of which there is always only one, and which does not need to be detected at runtime.**

These specifics then allow to run the init handlers for the `platform device` before anything else (including `FruDevice`).

```
if (GB200){
do_gb200_specific_things();
do_common_thing_on_bus(2)
} else if (TyanS7106){
do_tyan_specific_things();
do_common_thing_on_bus(3);
}
```

I like this code sample. Here's another:

```
void gb200_init(){
    do_gb200_specific_things();
    do_common_thing_on_bus(2)
}

init_routines = {
  {"GB200", gb200_init},
  {"TyanS7016, tyan_s7016_init},
};

init_routines[fixed_device]();
```

With this structure, init routines can be re-used for devices detected at runtime.

```
void onDeviceAdded(std::string& dynamic_device){

    if (init_routines.contains(dynamic_device)){
        init_routines[dynamic_device]();
    }

    logDeviceAdded(device);
}
```

